  constraint: [38;5;153mapp.js:6[39m says the API call is a stub ("would POST to API_ENDPOINT in a real app"), so there may be no
  backend to receive logs yet.

  - [1mConsole-only, both runtimes.[0m Cheapest, no dependency, no endpoint. Node side genuinely works. Browser side only
    helps when you can reproduce locally or talk a user through opening devtools — it does not give you a production
    trail. Cheap to change later; the call-site API stays the same if we add a transport.
  - [1mBuffer-and-ship on error (browser) + stdout JSON (Node).[0m Browser keeps the last N events in memory and POSTs the
    batch only when an error fires, so normal sessions cost nothing. Gives you the actual trail. Costs: needs a
    receiving endpoint, and it makes redaction load-bearing — anything mis-logged near that login form leaves the user's
    machine. Hard to undo once real user data starts flowing.
  - [1mThird-party service (Sentry etc.).[0m Most capability for least code, and solves trail + errors properly. Costs: an
    external dependency, sending user data to a vendor, and a decision that's well beyond "add logging."

  [1mMy recommendation: console-only now, but built behind a small logger interface designed so the transport can be added
[0m  [1mwithout touching a single call site.[0m You asked for a behavioral trail, and console-only doesn't fully deliver that in
  the browser — but the stub API says the receiving end doesn't exist yet, and I'd rather not design a shipping pipeline
  against an endpoint nobody has built. The interface is the part that's expensive to change; the transport is the part
  that's cheap to add once there's somewhere to send it.

